export const FINANCE_SYMMETRY: FleetText[] = [
  { domain: "adb-aiib", text: "UPA vs NDA (Union): AIIB did not exist before 2016, so the control is impossible for AIIB by construction; for ADB, the only period totals opened here are NDA-era (US$3bn 2018 \"highest since 1986\", US$3.9bn 2020, US$4.6bn 2021, US$2.6bn 2023) — UPA-era annual commitments were not fetched, so no Union-level comparison is made and the 2018–21 records are recorded as facts, not as a finding about the government. States: the same lens on ADB's top recipients returns MP (BJP), Rajasthan (INC 2018–23) and Bihar (JD(U)-BJP; MGB 2022–24) and ADB's own explanation (40–50% lower-income-state rule since CPS 2013–17); the AIIB list spans BJP-, INC-, DMK-, LDF-, TMC-, AAP- and YSRCP-run states. The lens produces no partisan picture at this resolution — which is a finding about the lens (state income category predicts lending; party does not, unnormalised). Chinese official finance vs Japanese ODA: China lends to India through AIIB only (no bilateral sovereign loans recorded here); Japan lends bilaterally (JICA, JPY 6.978 tn cumulative) and through ADB (LEAP, JFPR). Run on Japan, the \"foreign bank funds our infrastructure with strings\" lens gives an equally or more alarming picture — tied technology on MAHSR, Japanese-funded ADB windows co-lending to Indian private companies — which nobody circulates, because the alarm is about the lender's flag, not the terms." },
  { domain: "bilateral-china", text: "The brief's control is Chinese official finance to Indian private companies against Japanese ODA to Indian public projects, on the same fields. Run on both: Japan — sovereign borrower (GoI/DEA), 0.1–2.7% for 30–50 years, 10–15-year grace, disclosed conditions (MAHSR tied; most others untied), named executing agencies, an ex-ante evaluation per loan, zero defaults; China — private/SOE borrowers, LIBOR + 1.8–4.75%, 4–13-year maturities, security over plant and telecom licences, equipment-tied, Sinosure-insured, no public evaluation, three of the four largest borrowers insolvent. The lens that produces an alarming picture of Chinese money (collateral, litigation, promoter guarantees) produces none for Japanese money — but it also produces none for US Ex-Im and DFC, whose Sasan and First Solar loans are equally tied to home-country equipment and equally aimed at competing with China. Read symmetrically, 'tied export finance to Indian private borrowers' is what every ECA does; the discriminating facts about China are the borrowers' quality (the most leveraged groups of the 2008–12 boom) and the outcome (lender losses), not the lending. On the UPA/NDA control: the Chinese private lending peaks under UPA-II (2008–2013) and the JICA MAHSR/metro surge under NDA (2017–2026), but both are lender-and-borrower commercial timing (3G auction 2010, Shinkansen decision 2015), and neither government signed the private loans; no partisan gap survives the date test." },
  { domain: "contracts", text: "Lens: 'who wins, how concentrated, how many bid, how often single-source' applied to the lender-funded family and to the domestic Union register (research/raw/tenders-centre.json, 88 awards 2019–2026). Lender-funded: top winner by value L&T 20.3% (WB) / 35.1% (ADB RRTS); top five 62.5%; mean 5.6 bidders where published, 0 single-bid awards, 2.2% direct selection (consultancies only). Domestic: top winner by count Adani group 17/88 (19%); by value PSU-GoI 30.5% and Reliance 26.0% (spectrum auctions dominate); bidder counts known for 16/88 (mean 4.25); no sole-bidder awards flagged; 59/88 are auctions where 'concentration' is a revenue-share outcome, not a contract. Reading: lender-funded procurement shows more published competition and a lower observed top-winner share than the domestic register, but the domestic register's bidder data are so sparse that the difference is partly one of disclosure. The lens produces an 'L&T dominates' picture for lender money and an 'Adani/Reliance dominate' picture for domestic money; both dissolve on inspection into a handful of pre-qualifiable firms splitting large packages by L1, and neither register contains a documented single-bidder award. The alarming-looking fact common to both is opacity after award, not rigging before it." },
  { domain: "debt-imf-people", text: "Applied to the Union-level control (UPA 2004-2014 vs NDA 2014-2026): the same World-Bank-sourced lens shows external debt growing under both dispensations without a partisan discontinuity that survives a base-rate check — nominal stock CAGR was higher in the UPA decade (approx 14.0%/yr, 2004-2014) than the NDA decade (approx 4.6%/yr, 2014-2024), which a naive reading could spin as \"NDA more prudent,\" but the innocent reading (claim debt:c026) is that compounding off a roughly 3.7x-larger 2014 base mechanically produces a lower percentage growth rate even under identical policy, and the 2004-2014 window also contains the 2013 \"taper tantrum\" rupee shock, which moves a current-US$ figure independent of borrowing decisions. On the personnel side, RBI Governors and Finance Ministers were appointed by both UPA and NDA governments across this file's window without any observable pattern in the debt trajectory tracking the appointing party. This file could NOT run the two other mandatory controls (BJP-run vs opposition-run state borrowing; Chinese finance to private companies vs Japanese ODA to public projects) — both require data this file did not fetch and are left to the companion finance-fleet files, recorded as gaps above." },
  { domain: "literature", text: "UPA vs NDA: the strongest documented case of external policy conditions shaping Indian law is the 1991 IMF/World Bank package under a Congress government (Rao/Singh), and the World Bank's own evaluator says even then 'Conditionality was used sparingly'; the NDA-era DPOs (Kerala 2019, PMGKY and MSME 2020, low-carbon 2023 — worldbank.json) carry prior actions drawn from the government's own programmes. The 'strings' lens applied to the UPA control produces the more alarming picture, which is a finding about the lens: it detects crisis-year borrowing, not party. BJP-run vs opposition-run states: the one litigated case of Union restriction (Kerala, 2023–24) sits beside a 2019 IDA-blend DPO to the same opposition-run state, and the Bank's 1990s focus states spanned TDP, BJP, BSP, Janata Dal, Congress and BJD governments; no partisan pattern is documented, and the analytic gap remains open until the state-wise EAP table is built. Chinese vs Japanese money: the 'closed club' and 'capture' lenses run on the Japanese control produce a literal contractual closed club (tied MAHSR procurement) and a 50-year creditor relationship with a Union PSU — more concentrated than anything documented for Chinese credit to Indian private firms — so the alarm the lens raises for China is a property of the lens unless control rights are shown. Doing Business: the countries the audit names are China, Azerbaijan, Saudi Arabia and the UAE; India is reviewed and cleared." },
  { domain: "worldbank-projects", text: "Same lens on both eras, computed from this file's non-pipeline loan claims (Dropped projects and grant-only trust-fund operations excluded): UPA (approvals 2004-05-22 to 2014-05-25) 146 claims, US$36,036 m, ₹1,75,357 crore at approval-year rates — US$3,604 m a year; NDA (2014-05-26 to 2026-09-26) 180 claims, US$44,863 m, ₹3,38,680 crore — US$3,637 m a year; before 2004-05-22, 502 claims, US$60,794 m, ₹1,33,485 crore. Read the ₹ series with care: the rupee moved from about ₹45 to ₹87 per US$ between the two eras, so nominal ₹ totals rise mechanically; compare the US$ column, and against India's GDP and external-debt stock (debt domain) before calling any gap a finding. State denominator: 28 states/UTs carry a state attribution (borrower state government, else the implementing agency's state, else a state named in the project title — not a lender field); US$52,957 m (₹2,90,657 crore) of US$141,798 m is attributable to a state at all, the rest is Union or national programmes. Top states by US$: Tamil Nadu 10% (US$5,472 m, 28 claims), Andhra Pradesh 10% (US$5,398 m, 30 claims), Maharashtra 9% (US$4,995 m, 25 claims), Karnataka 9% (US$4,673 m, 33 claims), Uttar Pradesh 8% (US$4,244 m, 21 claims), Gujarat 7% (US$3,604 m, 20 claims). Whether BJP-run or opposition-run states borrowed more in a given year needs the ruling-party-by-year table (people domain) joined on approval date: this file does not carry it, so no party share is asserted here." },
  { domain: "worldbank", text: "Same lens on UPA 2004–14: 177 approved projects, US$37.5bn, 31% state-attributed. Per-capita ratios under UPA were more dispersed than under NDA (HP 9.7x, Uttarakhand 4.8x, Karnataka 2.1x, TN 2.1x, Odisha 2.0x vs Gujarat 0.29x, AP 0.35x, WB 0.49x). Under NDA the dispersion is similar in shape (Mizoram 7x, HP 6.3x, Assam 3.3x vs Chhattisgarh 0.18x, Odisha 0.39x). The two largest movers are Gujarat (up) and Odisha/Karnataka (down) — one aligned, two opposition-run for most of the period, and Karnataka BJP-run 2019–23. The lens produces an 'alarming' picture in both periods and for both blocs, which is a finding about the lens: small states and pipeline timing dominate. DPL conditionality: UPA-era DPLs (e.g. 2009 Banking Sector Support US$2bn, the largest since 2000) were not read here — a gap, not a finding." },
];

