        "claimId": "adb-aiib:c051",
        "domain": "adb-aiib",
        "lens": "both",
        "refuted": false,
        "recommendedTier": "analytic",
        "sourceCheck": "partially",
        "denialFound": "No accusation is being made, so there is nothing to deny. The response on record is from an Indian official quoted by Mint in July 2020 and reproduced in The Diplomat (2021-06-14): \"We are not taking money from China but from the multilateral development bank. I don't see any structural change in our engagement with AIIB because of our tensions with China.\" https://thediplomat.com/2021/06/amid-tensions-with-china-india-proceeding-with-aiib-funded-projects/ . The record also cites SCMP (2021-06-04): AIIB president Jin Liqun said loans to India were maintained. I did not re-open the SCMP article.",
        "innocentReading": "The pandemic facility was open to every AIIB member, and approvals bank-wide doubled in 2020–21. India's share stayed near 20–28%, so India's totals simply tracked the bank. The record's version of this reading has two errors. First, India's COVID Crisis Recovery Facility (CRF) loans were US$2.25bn in four loans, not US$1.75bn in three: CARES US$750m (2020), Health Systems US$500m (2020), Social Protection US$500m (2021), and Vaccines US$500m (2022, tagged 'CRF-Public Health'). So 2022 is inflated by the facility as well. Second, excluding CRF, the series is 820 → 700 → 1,446 → 1,369 → 1,105 → 1,058. Non-COVID approvals in 2020 were slightly below 2019. The 2023–24 dip happened while bank-wide approvals rose, so it is India-specific in relative terms. 'India's pivot to ADB/World Bank policy loans' is not sourced anywhere in the record.",
        "reason": "The core analytic claim survives. AIIB's own primary data file reproduces every yearly figure, and India's share of AIIB approvals held or rose in 2020–22. That makes 'no post-Galwan slowdown in approvals' a sound analytic reading. The Diplomat independently found that \"I did not come across evidence of any project being halted due to government intervention.\" Analytic is the right tier: the numbers are documented, but the 'no slowdown' conclusion is an inference, and approval data cannot show pipeline attrition. I could not refute the claim, but its benefit clause and innocent reading have factual errors that must be fixed. The ≈US$5.8bn credited to 'the Union and states' includes US$400m of private nonsovereign deals. 'Drew' overstates what the data shows, since approvals are not disbursements. 'Bilateral ties curtailed' goes beyond the cited source, which says only 'a few' Chinese investments were halted and that 'New Delhi has done little' on imports. The CRF amount is understated.",
        "corrections": [
          "1) benefit.how: change 'drew ≈US$5.8bn of AIIB approvals' to 'had ≈US$5.37bn of sovereign AIIB approvals in 2020–22 (US$5.77bn including US$0.4bn nonsovereign to HDFC, Enel, Kotak fund and a solar IPP)'. 'Drew' should become 'were approved for'. 2) benefit.amountCr: 42949 is based on 5,765; recompute on sovereign 5,365 at the same rate (~₹74.5/US$), giving ≈39,970. 3) innocentReading: change 'US$1.75bn in three loans' to 'US$2.25bn in four loans (2020 CARES 750, 2020 Health 500, 2021 Social Protection 500, 2022 Vaccines 500)'; change '2020–21 totals inflated' to '2020–22 totals inflated'. Either remove 'India's pivot to larger ADB/World Bank policy loans' or source it. Note that non-CRF approvals in 2020 (US$700m) were below 2019 (US$820m). 4) d: add India's share of AIIB approvals (20.1 / 21.0 / 20.9 / 28.3% for 2019–22; 9.8 / 13.1% for 2023–24) against bank-wide totals (4.08 / 9.30 / 9.30 / 6.62 / 11.25 / 8.09 US$bn). The share is the non-trivial comparison, and it shows the 2023–24 dip is India-specific because bank-wide lending rose. 5) Change the phrase 'bilateral economic ties with China were curtailed' to 'some Chinese investment was restricted or halted (The Diplomat: \"only a few\")', or add a primary source such as Press Note 3 (2020). 6) Add the Mint/Diplomat official quote as the response field. 7) Note in d that year grouping uses the file's 'date' field, not the URL path year (HORC and Assam roads carry 2020 paths but were approved in 2022)."
        ],
        "applied": [
          "recorded"
        ]
