        "claimId": "bilateral-china:c039",
        "domain": "bilateral-china",
        "lens": "both",
        "refuted": false,
        "recommendedTier": "reported",
        "sourceCheck": "confirmed",
        "denialFound": "No denial or contrary reporting was found. Neither CDB nor RCom disputes the withdrawal. RCom's regulatory filing that day said it was working \"expeditiously\" to close asset monetisation in \"January-March\". There is nothing to deny: the event is a matter of court record, and the article is written with both parties' positions stated. A second independent outlet was not checked because the session's web-search budget had run out.",
        "innocentReading": "A secured creditor holding 37% of secured debt drops an IBC petition before admission because the debtor has a near-term plan (the Jio sale by March 2018) to pay lenders. That recovery is worth more than going into CIRP, and CDB kept the right to refile. ET also reports possible out-of-court settlement talks, including Chinese lenders reportedly taking 70% in Dhirubhai Ambani Knowledge City. That is ordinary creditor bargaining, not favouritism. The later RCom insolvency (c040) and the Chinese banks' 2019-20 suits against Anil Ambani's personal guarantee show that the forbearance was conditional.",
        "reason": "I opened the single cited source (ET, 5 January 2018, by Devina Sengupta) with curl. It states every material element: the Friday 2018-01-05 withdrawal, Rs 11,460 cr dues, \"37% of RCom's total secured debt\", \"accommodating\" the asset-monetisation plan running to March 2018, the right to refile, the NCLT filing made the previous November, and the sale to Jio for under Rs 24,000 cr as part of the plan. There are no errors of date, identity or amount. It is one credible outlet with named sourcing (counsel in open court, the bench, and RCom's filing), so \"reported\" is the right tier. It is not \"documented\", because the NCLT withdrawal order was not read and no second outlet was confirmed.",
        "corrections": [
          "1) Summary text: change \"to accommodate RCom's asset sale to Reliance Jio\" to \"to accommodate RCom's asset-monetisation plan (wireless and real-estate assets, incl. spectrum/tower/fibre/MCN sale to Reliance Jio for under ₹24,000 cr) running to March 2018\". The source frames the Jio sale as part of a wider plan, not the sole reason. 2) Attribute the \"accommodating\" rationale to CDB's counsel Darius Khambatta in the tribunal, not to CDB directly. 3) benefit.confidence is \"documented\" but the edge tier is \"reported\". Set it to \"reported\". 4) pred \"enforce\" is defined as agency→subject, and CDB is a creditor, not an agency. Either keep it with the lab making clear this is a creditor IBC petition that was withdrawn, or use a creditor-action predicate if the schema has one. 5) Optionally add ET's note of reported CDB and Chinese-lender talks on a 70% stake in Dhirubhai Ambani Knowledge City as context for a possible out-of-court settlement. Mark it \"reported (ET earlier report)\". 6) On c040 (outside this claim's scope): in Feb 2019 RCom's board decided to pursue resolution through NCLT, and admission came on Ericsson's petition in May 2019. Check the wording \"filed for bankruptcy\"."
        ],
