        "denialFound": "none found — not asked. For an analytic concentration edge that implies no wrongdoing, no response is required. My WebSearch budget ran out, so I did not search for contrary reporting.",
        "innocentReading": "L&T is the largest Indian EPC firm. It won the three EDFC-3 (P150158) works contracts as L1 in open RFB competitions where the notices list the rivals and their prices. On CP-303, L&T bid ₹2,870.99 cr against KEC-Longjian at ₹2,991 cr (4.2% higher), IL&FS JV at ₹2,996.5 cr, and GR Infra-Kyivmetrobud at ₹3,040 cr, among others. On the system contracts, KEC-Hitachi/Ansaldo bid 28–35% higher. On the comparable 2016 CP-301 and CP-302 packages, L&T was the L2 bidder and lost to GIL-TPL by 1.3% and 5.8%. Lumpy ₹700–2,900 cr packages split among a few pre-qualified firms by L1 fully explain a ~20% share.",
        "reason": "The core numbers reproduce from the WB procnotices API. L&T's lead-firm INR award total is exactly ₹6,076.46 cr. There are 370 award notices when PMGSY P124639 is read at rows=200, and 11 projects carry INR prices. But the claim text says EDFC-3 was won \"all three packages, rivals unpublished\", and the cited P150158 source contradicts this. Each of the three L&T award notices publishes its rivals. CP-303 (IN-DFCCIL-1848-CW-RFB) lists seven evaluated bidders with prices, e.g. \"GR INFRAPROJECT LIMITED ... PJSC KYIVMETROBUD ... Bid Price at Opening INR 30,400,000,000.00\". IN-DFCCIL-1846 and IN-DFCCIL-1847 list KEC-Ansaldo/Hitachi as evaluated at ₹997.7 cr and ₹1,012.6 cr, plus rejected Tata, Alstom, Kalindee and Texmaco bids. That false opacity phrase is load-bearing: it is the only part of the edge that insinuates something beyond size. The published fields show L&T was L1 by 4–28% in all three packages. Two lesser problems. First, the denominator is a truncation artifact: PMGSY was read at rows=200 of 430 notices. The full INR total is ₹31,234 cr (L&T 19.5%), and my re-parse of the same 370 notices gives ₹30,537 cr (19.9%) rather than ₹29,919 cr. Second, the \"1–5%\" loss margins are actually 1.3% and 5.8%. As written, the claim fails. Once corrected, it survives as an analytic edge.",
