        "innocentReading": "The boring explanation that fits the data: the gap is mainly the global cycle, shared by about 86% of peer emerging markets, together with slower US$ nominal GDP growth after 2014. Two further effects reduce the post-2014 dollar figure: the dollar strengthened, and the rupee depreciation shrank the US$ value of rupee-denominated external liabilities (FPI holdings of government securities, rupee NRI deposits). Growth and retrenchment in private corporate ECB/NRI borrowing, which is not sovereign policy, also moves the total. Scaled to GDP, the ratio rose under UPA and fell modestly under NDA. The difference is small, and the cyclical factors above can explain it.\n\nThe innocent reading as filed contains two errors:\n1. \"A lower 2014 base mechanically compounds faster\" (in the claim text) and \"compounding off a ~3.7x-larger base mechanically produces a lower percentage growth rate even under identical policy\" (in innocentReading and symmetryCheck) are false. CAGR does not depend on scale. Identical policy, meaning identical growth rates, gives an identical CAGR whatever the base. A base effect only exists if absolute increments are held fixed. Even then NDA added less in absolute terms: +$259bn against +$334bn under UPA.\n2. The 2013 taper tantrum points the wrong way. Rupee depreciation lowers the US$ value of rupee-denominated debt, so it depressed the UPA-window figure rather than inflating it.",
        "reason": "The numbers and the source are confirmed exactly, and \"analytic\" is the right tier for a comparison of our own making. But an analytic claim rests on its innocentReading, and the one given here is arithmetically false. It says a larger or smaller base \"mechanically\" changes CAGR, and the claim text even inverts which base is lower. It also gets the direction of the taper-tantrum valuation effect backwards. The edge is also pinned to the Ministry of Finance, although about two-thirds of the stock is non-sovereign. As written, the claim does not survive. It should be kept as analytic only after the rationale is replaced with the peer base rate (about 86%) and the debt/GDP scaling.",
