        "reason": "The headline shares (31% to 52%) survive only as a method-dependent analytic figure. The party half of the claim fails the date test. It says \"no party pattern\" and supports that with labels that are wrong at the approval dates. Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu are called \"southern opposition-run\", but most of AP's money (including the US$800m Amaravati programme) and most of TN's pre-2021 money was approved under NDA-allied governments. Chhattisgarh is called \"BJP-run\" below 0.6x, but every one of its NDA-era projects was approved under Congress. Most of the hill and north-eastern states above 1.5x were BJP- or NDA-governed when their loans were approved. The claim also miscounts the hill/north-eastern group as 7 of 11 when it is 8. If the ratios are joined to ruling party by approval date, they do not show the neutral mix the text describes. They lean NDA-aligned, and small-state denominators and pipeline timing explain that lean. So the defensible statement is \"party cannot be separated from state size and timing on this lens\", not \"no party pattern\". The claim as worded does not survive, and it needs correcting before it can stay analytic.",
