        "innocentReading": "Both AIIB loans were add-ons to programmes led by other banks. The health loan co-financed a World Bank project, approved 7 May. CARES co-financed ADB's US$1.5bn CARES programme, which ADB signed on 28 Apr 2020. Their approval dates followed AIIB's COVID-facility processing cycle, which began in April–May, before the clash. The money came from a multilateral bank in which India is the second-largest shareholder (7.6% per FPJ) and India's largest source of AIIB lending. It paid for health response and PMGKY cash transfers. Signing three days after Galwan reflects a timetable already under way, not a decision taken after the clash.",
        "reason": "The narrative survives as a two-sided \"contested\" record. The borrowing is on primary record: the AIIB pages show US$500m approved 2020-05-07 and US$750m approved 2020-06-16, and ADB PDS 54182-001 lists \"Co-financer AIIB: US$ 750 million\". The claimant's side and the counter-side are both put fairly. The source chain has one load-bearing fault. The headline figure \"₹9,200 crore\" appears in none of the four cited sources. FPJ, the only outlet the record credits with the rupee figures, gives only dollars: \"USD 1,250 million\". The ₹9,200 cr is an internal sum of c003 (₹5,521 cr) and c004 (₹3,676 cr). Both of those are mislabelled \"as stated by FPJ\" when they are our own conversions. The signing dates (05-08 and 06-19) rest on FPJ alone, and FPJ contradicts itself on amounts (\"USD 500 million and USD 250 million\" against a total of 1,250). Those dates are therefore reported, not documented. Finally, \"contested\" is a narrative calibration status, not a tier. Where one tier value is required, this is our own two-sided judgement, i.e. analytic, with the component loan edges documented (approvals) or reported (signings). These faults need correcting, but none of them overturns the narrative's verdict.",
