        "denialFound": "None found; not asked. No ADB, AIIB or ReNew response exists because no named party making the \"subsidy channel\" allegation has been identified. The narrative has no proponent source, which makes it a possible strawman, and the file should say so. Web search budget was exhausted this session, so I could not look for contrary reporting.",
        "innocentReading": "Domestic banks had hit their power-sector exposure limits and long-tenor debt for renewables was scarce (CPSFR para 16 and following). ADB's private-sector arm therefore took anchor equity in several early-stage platforms and lent to the same sponsors again as they grew. Offtake came from competitively auctioned SECI and state PPAs. ADB exited at a profit (ReNew 2018 above-average; Welspun 2016 satisfactory; Avaada 2021 successful). An equity investor earning above-average returns and exiting is the opposite of a subsidy on the equity leg. What remains unanswered is whether the debt pricing was below market, and no loan rate is in hand.",
        "reason": "The claim's own source says something different from the claim. The CPSFR sentence \"ADB exited the investment in 2016 with satisfactory returns\" is about Welspun Renewables; for ReNew the report says \"ADB exited the investment in 2018 with above-average financial returns.\" Separately, \"all recorded deals at market-based OCR pricing\" is not supported. Every loan claim in the file has ratePct null. Neither cited source (PDS 52126-001, the CPSFR) states pricing. Three of the deals include LEAP money, the JICA-funded Leading Asia's Private Infrastructure Fund, which is not OCR: US$25m in Avaada per the PDS, and LEAP portions in the 2016 ReNew SPVs and in Vyoman. The AIIB loan is not \"OCR\" at all. The overall judgment (the subsidy narrative is unsupported) probably survives, and the corrected facts, above-average returns and exits from all three anchor platforms, strengthen the counter. But the text as written contains a misattributed fact and a universal claim nobody checked, so it cannot pass. \"Speculative\" is not a contract tier. A narrative evaluation is our own inference, so the tier is analytic with an innocentReading.",
